[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Unit Testing, Stubbing, Mocking, and TDD in iOS (Swift)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Building robust, maintainable iOS applications requires a disciplined approach to testing. This tutorial explores XCTest, the default testing framework in Swift, alongside advanced concepts like stubbing, mocking, and Test-Driven Development (TDD)."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Introduction to XCTest"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"XCTest is Apple's built-in framework for writing unit tests, performance tests, and UI tests. A standard unit test case inherits from XCTestCase and contains methods prefixed with 'test'."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"import XCTest
@testable import YourAppModule

class CalculatorTests: XCTestCase {
    func testAddition() {
        let calculator = Calculator()
        let result = calculator.add(2, 3)
        XCTAssertEqual(result, 5, "Expected 2 + 3 to equal 5")
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Test-Driven Development (TDD)"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"TDD is a software development process relying on a short development cycle: write a failing test, write code to pass the test, and refactor the code. This is often summarized as Red-Green-Refactor."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Red: Write a test for a feature that does not exist yet and watch it fail."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Green: Write just enough production code to make the test pass."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Refactor: Clean up the code while ensuring all tests continue to pass."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Stubbing and Mocking"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"When unit testing, we want to isolate the unit of work. Dependencies like network managers or databases should be replaced with doubles: stubs (provide canned answers) and mocks (verify behavior)."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"protocol NetworkServiceProtocol {
    func fetchUserData(completion: @escaping (Result<Data, Error>) -> Void)
}

class MockNetworkService: NetworkServiceProtocol {
    var stubbedResult: Result<Data, Error>?
    var fetchUserDataCalled = false

    func fetchUserData(completion: @escaping (Result<Data, Error>) -> Void) {
        fetchUserDataCalled = true
        if let result = stubbedResult {
            completion(result)
        }
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Testing with the Mock"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Now we can inject our mock service into the ViewModel to test how it handles success and failure states without making real network requests."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"class UserViewModelTests: XCTestCase {
    func testFetchUserSuccess() {
        let mockService = MockNetworkService()
        let expectedData = "{\"name\":\"John\"}".data(using: .utf8)!
        mockService.stubbedResult = .success(expectedData)

        let viewModel = UserViewModel(networkService: mockService)
        viewModel.loadUser()

        XCTAssertTrue(mockService.fetchUserDataCalled)
        XCTAssertEqual(viewModel.userName, "John")
    }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"By mastering XCTest, practicing TDD, and utilizing stubs and mocks, you can write highly reliable, fast, and maintainable Swift codebases."}]
